gradients: [('The prompt may have failed to correctly classify these examples due to a lack of specific guidance on interpreting common subjective claims or contentious statements that can be interpreted in multiple ways. For instance, phrases like "rigged" or "openly stated" can reflect perceptions rather than verifiable facts, leading to ambiguity in classification. Additionally, the prompt may not sufficiently emphasize the importance of distinguishing between opinion-based assertions and factual claims, which could result in misclassifications, especially in politically charged contexts. Lastly, the requirement for cross-referencing with reliable sources might not have adequately accounted for the nuances of each situation, leading to an underestimation of the inherent biases or rhetorical strategies present in the statements being evaluated.', '## Example 1\nText: "Statement: The Colorado caucus system for selecting Republican delegates is rigged.\nJob title: President-Elect\nState: New York\nParty: republican\nContext: a TV interview"\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No\n\n## Example 2\nText: "Statement: The Muslim Brotherhood has openly stated they want to declare war on Israel.\nJob title: \nState: \nParty: none\nContext: a comment on his television show"\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No\n\n## Example 3\nText: "Statement: One of the biggest polluters in our country is the Milwaukee Metropolitan Sewerage District.\nJob title: Member of Congress\nState: Wisconsin\nParty: republican\nContext: a discussion at a town hall meeting"\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No\n\n## Example 4\nText: "Statement: Fifty-three percent of graduates from liberal arts colleges these days cant find jobs.\nJob title: U.S. Senator\nState: Georgia\nParty: republican\nContext: a speech"\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No')]
