gradients: [('The prompt may have led to incorrect predictions due to a lack of emphasis on the verification of specific claims made within each statement. While it outlines a thorough process for analysis, it might not prioritize the fact-checking of the key assertions that directly relate to the labels provided. For example, in the cases where the statements included quantifiable claims or specific accusations, the model may not have adequately identified these critical details for evaluation against factual data. Additionally, the context of the statements, such as the motivations of the speakers and the environment in which they were made, may not have been sufficiently weighted in the analysis, leading to misclassification as "not misleading" when they actually contain misleading elements. This indicates that the prompt may need to be adjusted to place greater importance on the factual accuracy and the verification of the core claims made in each statement.', '## Example 1\nText: "Statement: Thurbert Baker kept a teen in jail "who didn\'t even commit a felony."\nJob title: \nState: \nParty: none\nContext: a video"\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No\n\n## Example 2\nText: "Statement: "The majority actually want single-payer health care."\nJob title: Documentary filmmaker\nState: \nParty: none\nContext: an appearance on Larry King Live"\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No\n\n## Example 3\nText: "Statement: Says Patrick Murphy advocated for cuts to Social Security and Medicare.\nJob title: \nState: \nParty: republican\nContext: a campaign website"\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No\n\n## Example 4\nText: "Statement: Says 74 percent of small-business people believe that Obamacare is a bad idea.\nJob title: Radio talk show host\nState: \nParty: talk-show-host\nContext: an interview"\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No')]
